# Apache config for the plain static production deploy (Network Solutions
# or any other Apache/Unix host). Harmless where it doesn't apply (GitHub
# Pages ignores .htaccess entirely; the WordPress shell theme serves its
# dist/ folder's real files directly, so this only adds caching there).

# Hashed assets (js/css/images) are safe to cache forever — their filename
# changes whenever the content does. index.html must never be cached, or
# a stale copy will keep pointing at a JS/CSS bundle that's since been
# deleted by a later deploy, breaking the site until a hard refresh.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <FilesMatch "\.(js|css|jpg|jpeg|png|webp|gif|svg|ico|woff2?)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
  </FilesMatch>
  <FilesMatch "\.html$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# Block public access to the archived pre-migration WordPress backup —
# it's real files sitting inside the web root (this account has no
# writable folder outside it), so without this rule they'd be directly
# reachable by URL even though nothing links to them.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteRule ^_wp_backup_20260921/ - [F,L]
</IfModule>

# SPA fallback: any request that isn't a real file or directory (e.g.
# /pine-woods, /majestic) gets index.html, and the app's own client-side
# routing (App.tsx) decides what to render — including showing its own
# NotFoundPage for anything that doesn't match a real route. No
# RewriteBase — omitting it lets Apache infer it from this file's own
# directory, so the same rule works whether this is deployed at the
# site root or in a subfolder like /preview/.
#
# A tighter, whitelist-based version of this rule plus an ErrorDocument
# 404 directive (so unknown paths get a real HTTP 404 status instead of
# this 200) was tried and reverted — it broke the live homepage itself
# on this host, even though it tested clean against a local Apache
# instance, most likely an AllowOverride/ErrorDocument support
# difference on Network Solutions' actual server config that local
# testing couldn't catch. Revisit with more cautious, incremental
# testing (e.g. against the /preview/ subfolder first) before trying
# again — don't just restore the whitelist rule from git history
# without re-verifying it against the real host.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On
  RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
  RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
  RewriteRule ^ index.html [L]
</IfModule>
